t equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}f(x)=-1\\ne 1=f(-1)\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. In order for the function to be continuous at $x=-1,$ it must be both left-continuous and right-continuous. However, we have already determined that the function is neither left-continuous nor right-continuous at this point.\n\nIn conclusion, none of the statements are true." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-38546/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "81247553-750f-5bf7-a1c4-698ad63b5ae4"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-38546"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-38548"] :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is false. The function is not left-continuous at $x=-1,$ since the left-sided limit is not equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x)=1\\ne 2=f(-1)\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. The function is not right-continuous at $x=-1,$ since the right-sided limit is not equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}f(x)=3\\ne 2=f(-1)\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. In order for the function to be continuous at $x=-1,$ it must be both left-continuous and right-continuous. However, we have already determined that the function is neither left-continuous nor right-continuous at this point.\n\nIn conclusion, none of the statements are true." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-73105/selection/93198c2e65472148f49c73a47d70892e6efd0b46acbb489ee2afeea42a4a8c25" :answer/id #uuid "0d86bdee-d069-5f1e-9098-b1f4fa1975c1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-73105/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "93d0a04d-24bc-59a6-829b-e34bfea4d50b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure